# The Geometry of Participation

## Proclus’s *Elements of Theology* through process inexternalism

The *Elements of Theology* is not primarily a cosmological story about an entity called “the One” emitting lower entities. It is a proof-system constructed to answer a more exact problem:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{How can something be genuinely distinct without being}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{independent of the conditions that make it what it is?}
}
]

Its deepest object is neither substance nor emanation. It is **dependent distinction**.

Proclus wants to demonstrate simultaneously that:

1. multiplicity is real;
2. multiplicity is impossible without unity;
3. effects are distinct from causes;
4. effects remain internally related to their causes;
5. individuality therefore does not require self-grounding independence;
6. perfection consists in an entity actively restoring its relation to its source.

The work consists of 211 chained propositions, moving from unity and causation through participation, self-constitution, eternity, gods, intellect and soul. The first half develops the general metaphysical machinery; the second applies it to henads, intellects and souls. It is modelled structurally on mathematical demonstration, though its proofs depend on metaphysical assumptions that are not themselves formally neutral.

The whole book can be compressed into one circuit:

[
\boxed{
\text{unity}
\rightarrow
\text{participated determination}
\rightarrow
\text{distinct participant}
\rightarrow
\text{return through recognition}.
}
]

---

# I. The opening argument: unity is prior to identity

## Propositions 1–6

Proclus begins:

[
\text{every multitude participates unity}.
]

This does not initially mean that every multitude participates a supernatural cosmic object.

It means that anything countable, identifiable or intelligible as a manifold must possess enough unity to constitute:

* one manifold;
* determinate members;
* stable relations among those members.

A collection that possessed no unity whatsoever would not be a collection. Its members would not even remain identifiable as members. Proclus therefore argues that every multitude is both:

[
\text{one}
]

and:

[
\text{not-one}.
]

It is one insofar as it forms a determinate whole. It is not the One itself because its unity coexists with internal plurality.

The argument’s valid core is:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Determinate}(X)
\Rightarrow
\operatorname{Unified}(X).
}
]

This is stronger than the trivial statement that we call things “one.” Proclus is claiming that real determinacy requires a principle of coherence:

[
X
=

\operatorname{OneWhole}
\left(
x_1,\ldots,x_n;
R
\right).
]

The elements must be held within some relation (R) that makes them one system rather than an arbitrary list.

## The first overreach

The following inference is not yet compulsory:

[
\operatorname{Unified}(X)
\Rightarrow
\exists!\mathbf 1
]

where (\mathbf 1) is one universal metaphysical source of all unity.

The propositions establish a **unity requirement** more securely than they establish the One as a separate first principle.

In our terminology:

[
\boxed{
\text{local determinacy requires unity;}
}
]

but:

[
\boxed{
\text{local unity does not yet prove one universal subject.}
}
]

This is where Proclus begins more cautiously than Abhinavagupta. The first principle is not yet consciousness, mind or agent. It is that without which nothing could be a determinate thing at all.

