# II. Why the One becomes the Good

## Propositions 7–13

Proclus then connects unity with preservation.

A thing exists as the thing it is only while its organization remains sufficiently unified. Disintegration destroys its determinate being:

[
\operatorname{LossOfUnity}(X)
\rightarrow
\operatorname{LossOfForm}(X).
]

Unity is therefore not merely numerical. It is the condition that preserves an entity’s characteristic organization.

Proclus consequently identifies the Good with the One:

[
\mathbf G=\mathbf 1.
]

The reasoning is:

1. the good of a thing is what preserves and perfects it;
2. what preserves it as this thing is its unity;
3. therefore unity is good;
4. the unqualified source of unity is the unqualified Good.

Proposition 13 explicitly identifies goodness with unification and treats falling away from unity as falling away from the Good.

## The profound insight

The good is not initially moral approval.

It is:

[
\boxed{
\text{successful realization of a thing’s coherent form}.
}
]

This anticipates biological normativity:

[
\operatorname{GoodFor}(X,s)
===========================

\text{state }s\text{ supports the continued realization of }X.
]

A living organism’s good is inseparable from maintaining the organization that makes it that organism.

## The unresolved leap

Unity and viability are not identical.

Some unified entities may be destructive, unstable or indifferent to continued existence. Proclus assumes:

[
\text{more unified}
\Rightarrow
\text{more perfect}.
]

That principle structures the entire hierarchy, but it is not independently proven.

Our framework therefore retains:

[
\text{unity is constitutive of determinate existence}
]

while treating:

[
\text{maximum unity}=\text{maximum value}
]

as a further thesis.

