# IV. The master theorem of participation

## Proposition 23

This is probably the single most important proposition for our project.

Proclus distinguishes:

[
\boxed{
\text{unparticipated}
\rightarrow
\text{participated}
\rightarrow
\text{participant}.
}
]

Suppose a universal principle (U) were directly and identically present in every participant.

Then either:

* (U) would become divided among its participants;
* or every participant would contain numerically the same complete token of (U);
* or the distinction between universal cause and local realization would collapse.

Proclus introduces a mediation.

### Unparticipated principle

[
U
]

The universal source that remains undiminished and is not possessed as a private property by any one participant.

### Participated term

[
u_i
]

A determinate communication of (U), adapted to a particular order or participant.

### Participant

[
P_i
]

The entity whose organization receives and realizes (u_i).

Thus:

[
U
\rightarrow
u_i
\rightarrow
P_i.
]

Proposition 23 states that an unparticipated cause produces participated terms through which participants receive it.

## Why this matters

This directly answers the error:

[
\text{common source}
\Rightarrow
\text{one empirical subject}.
]

No.

For Proclus:

[
\operatorname{Source}(P_i)
==========================

# \operatorname{Source}(P_j)

U
]

does not imply:

[
P_i=P_j.
]

Nor does it imply:

[
\operatorname{Field}(P_i)
=========================

\operatorname{Field}(P_j).
]

The source remains universal precisely because it is not exhausted by any one local participation.

## Our reconstruction

Participatory inexternalism can now be expressed:

[
M_0
\rightarrow
m_i
\rightarrow
A_i,
]

where:

* (M_0): common manifest ground;
* (m_i): participant-relative actualization;
* (A_i): physically individuated aperture;
* (D_i): intrinsic phenomenal presentation of (A_i).

Then:

[
D_i
\equiv_{\mathrm{inext}}
A_i
]

and optionally:

[
D_i
===

\pi_{A_i}(M_0).
]

This preserves common ground without collapsing local fields.

Proclus therefore offers a cleaner model of plurality than simple cosmic-subject idealism.

