# V. Remaining, procession and return

## Propositions 30–35

The most famous Proclean triad is:

[
\boxed{
\text{remaining}
\rightarrow
\text{procession}
\rightarrow
\text{return}.
}
]

Proposition 35 states that every caused being remains in its cause, proceeds from it and reverts toward it.

This is often misunderstood as a temporal sequence:

1. the effect begins inside the cause;
2. leaves;
3. travels back.

That is not the primary meaning.

The triad describes three simultaneous dimensions of dependent existence.

## Remaining — monē

[
e\in C_{\mathrm{causal}}.
]

The effect remains in the cause because the cause continues to contain the principle of its existence.

A living form remains in its developmental organization even while its material components change.

A thought remains grounded in the cognitive system that produces it.

A local participant remains dependent upon conditions exceeding its present appearance.

## Procession — prohodos

[
e\neq C.
]

The effect acquires its own determinate existence.

Procession is distinction.

Without procession, the effect would be merely the cause under another name.

## Return — epistrophē

[
e\rightarrow C
]

not as spatial motion but as orientation toward the principle from which the effect receives being and perfection.

A being completes itself by realizing the causal character it receives.

The return is therefore normative:

[
\boxed{
\text{the origin of being is also the measure of flourishing}.
}
]

## Recognition as return

Recognition can be interpreted as a reflexive form of epistrophē:

[
\mathcal R_i:
P_i
\rightarrow
\operatorname{Know}
\left(
P_i\text{ as dependent upon }C
\right).
]

The participant does not cease being distinct.

It ceases treating distinction as independence.

That is the deepest Proclean formula for recognition:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{return is not destruction of difference;}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{it is difference becoming transparent to its source.}
}
]

This is more precise than “merging with the One.”

