# VI. Self-constitution without absolute independence

## Propositions 40–51

Proclus next develops the notion of the self-constituted:

[
\textit{authypostaton}.
]

A self-constituted being is not absolutely uncaused.

It has a higher cause, but unlike wholly dependent products, it possesses an internal principle through which it:

* preserves itself;
* returns upon itself;
* acts from itself;
* maintains its characteristic unity.

Propositions 40–41 distinguish entities existing entirely in another from beings that stand in themselves and are self-subsistent.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\text{self-constituted}
\neq
\text{self-created from nothing}.
}
]

Instead:

[
\operatorname{SelfConstituted}(X)
=================================

\operatorname{Caused}(X)
\land
\operatorname{InternalPrincipleOfPersistence}(X).
]

## This is astonishingly close to modern autonomy

A living process:

* depends on energy, matter and environment;
* is not externally assembled at every instant;
* uses internal organization to maintain itself;
* repairs deviations;
* produces the conditions of its continued operation.

In constructor-theoretic language, it approximately implements maintenance and restoration tasks.

But Proclus would still distinguish biological self-maintenance from full self-constitution, because he associates true self-reversion with incorporeality and soul.

## Where we disagree

Proclus argues:

[
\text{self-reversion}
\Rightarrow
\text{incorporeality}.
]

His reasoning is that a body is extended into spatial parts, whereas genuine self-reversion requires one and the same entity to be both subject and object of its activity.

Modern process theory can instead propose distributed reflexive organization:

[
X
=

{x_1,\ldots,x_n}
]

such that:

[
X
\rightarrow
\widehat X
\rightarrow
\operatorname{Control}(X).
]

The whole system can regulate itself without being spatially simple.

Proclus’s self-constitution is therefore an ancestor of our aperture concept, but not yet a viable physical account of consciousness.

