# VII. Three modes of existence

## Proposition 65

One of Proclus’s most powerful distinctions is that the same reality can exist in three modes:

[
\boxed{
\text{causally}
\quad
\text{formally}
\quad
\text{participatively}.
}
]

A property (F) exists:

### In its cause

[
F_C
]

in a more comprehensive and productive manner.

### In itself

[
F_F
]

as the determinate character of the thing whose form it is.

### In its participant

[
F_P
]

as a received and restricted realization.

The example given in modern summaries is life:

* life exists causally in intellective principle;
* formally in soul or life itself;
* participatively in living organisms.

## Why this is philosophically explosive

Identity of character does not imply identity of mode.

Suppose manifestation exists:

[
M_{\mathrm{ground}},
\quad
M_{\mathrm{aperture}},
\quad
M_{\mathrm{content}}.
]

These need not be three separate substances. Nor are they numerically interchangeable.

The same principle can exist:

[
\text{universally},
\quad
\text{locally},
\quad
\text{as a determinate occurrence}.
]

This allows us to formalize:

[
D_i
\equiv_{\mathrm{mode},i}
M_0
]

without claiming:

[
D_i
\equiv_{\mathrm{token}}
M_0.
]

That is precisely the distinction needed to make universal manifestation compatible with local privacy.

