# VIII. “All things are in all things”

## Proposition 103

Proclus states:

[
\boxed{
\text{all things are in all things, but in each according}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{to that thing’s characteristic mode}.
}
]

Being contains life and intellect in the mode of being.

Life contains being and intellect vitally.

Intellect contains being and life intellectively.

This is not the flat claim:

[
A=B=C.
]

It is:

[
A
=

\operatorname{Mode}_A(A,B,C),
]

[
B
=

\operatorname{Mode}_B(A,B,C),
]

[
C
=

\operatorname{Mode}_C(A,B,C).
]

Every level expresses the whole relational pattern according to its own dominant principle.

## Multiscale interpretation

An organism contains cellular agency organismically.

A cell contains organismic constraints cellularly.

A brain contains bodily regulation neurally.

The body contains neural processes metabolically and behaviorally.

This is not mystical identity. It is modal nesting:

[
\boxed{
\text{the whole is present in the parts as constraint;}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{the parts are present in the whole as organized powers.}
}
]

This is a better ontology of multiscale agency than either:

* reductionism, where only the lowest scale is real;
* holism, where parts disappear into the whole.

Proclus permits genuine levels because the same principle is transformed by the mode of its recipient.

