# IX. The gods as irreducible unities

## Propositions 113–165

At proposition 113, Proclus begins the section on divine henads.

The gods are not additional objects placed between the One and the universe. They solve a structural problem.

The One must remain:

* absolutely one;
* beyond determinate being;
* not internally divided.

Yet reality displays multiple irreducible causal orders.

Proclus therefore posits henads:

[
H_1,H_2,\ldots,H_n.
]

Each henad is:

* a self-complete unity;
* participated by a distinctive causal series;
* united with every other henad without becoming identical to it;
* a way in which divine unity becomes causally communicable.

Propositions 113–114 characterize the gods as self-complete unities, while later propositions explain that more remote participants receive them through mediating principles.

## Why henads matter for consciousness

Proclus refuses the idea that one highest principle must directly perform every local causal act.

There are plural sources of ordered identity:

[
\mathbf 1
\rightarrow
{H_a}
\rightarrow
{\text{causal series}}.
]

Each henad unifies a whole series without becoming one generic universal.

This provides a metaphysical model of **polycentric unity**:

[
\boxed{
\text{plural centres can share the One without}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{becoming fragments of one psychological ego}.
}
]

This is a profound contrast with the strongest Śaiva universal-subject claim.

Abhinavagupta ultimately identifies all agency with one universal consciousness.

Proclus places the One beyond subjecthood and allows a plurality of divine unities beneath it.

## From our position

We do not need to affirm literal henads.

But they teach a crucial anti-reduction principle:

[
\boxed{
\text{unity need not be monolithic}.
}
]

There may be:

* one universal condition of unity;
* many irreducible causal unities;
* many local participants;
* many private fields.

The One does not have to be a cosmic subject that owns every experience.

