# XV. What survives philosophical pressure

## Strongest results

### 1. Determinacy requires unity

[
\operatorname{Determinate}(X)
\Rightarrow
\operatorname{Unified}(X).
]

### 2. Difference requires both dependence and distinction

A genuine effect cannot be wholly identical to or wholly alien from its cause.

### 3. Common source does not imply token identity

[
\operatorname{Ground}(D_i)
==========================

\operatorname{Ground}(D_j)
\not\Rightarrow
D_i=D_j.
]

### 4. Participation requires mediation

Universal principles must be received according to participant-specific modes.

### 5. Self-constitution need not mean absolute independence

An entity may internally maintain itself while remaining caused and environmentally dependent.

### 6. Identity is mode-sensitive

[
F_{\mathrm{causal}}
\neq
F_{\mathrm{formal}}
\neq
F_{\mathrm{participated}},
]

even though all are expressions of (F).

### 7. Recognition preserves distinction

Reversion is fulfilled difference, not cancellation of difference.

