# XVI. Where Proclus exceeds his proofs

The Euclidean presentation can make controversial assumptions appear inevitable.

Several major transitions remain vulnerable.

## From local unity to the One

[
\forall X;
\operatorname{Unified}(X)
]

does not straightforwardly entail:

[
\exists!\mathbf 1
]

as an independently existing metaphysical principle.

## From unity to goodness

Unity may be necessary for existence without every increase in unity being an increase in goodness.

## From causal dependence to universal hierarchy

Not every form of dependence needs to fit a single linear order of higher and lower causes.

## From self-reversion to incorporeality

Distributed embodied systems may possess genuine reflexive regulation.

## From metaphysical participation to henads

The henads are a sophisticated solution to the One–many problem, but not a deductively forced one.

## From processual unity to immortal soul

The persistence of organization does not establish the survival of an incorporeal personal substance.

Proclus’s system is therefore not a completed proof of Neoplatonism.

It is a remarkably exact map of the explanatory obligations any relational or participatory ontology must satisfy.

